!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Paths
description: the colon grammar — one meaning for the colon everywhere
- |
  Colon is THE key marker of the JSON/YAML family: `key: value` means "this key leads
  to that value". A path is the same statement, chained — so the path separator is the
  same character, and `a: b: c` reads "key, leads to key, leads to key". Canonical
  block styling writes the colon with a space (`: `); the space is optional on input
  and absent in flow; machine surfaces — store keys, API paths — use the compact form
  (`:team:alice:age`).
- |
  A path is a sequence of **portions**. A portion selects a string key by name, and a
  `[n]` suffix selects the integer key n — a position: `: some: path[12]: with:
  ordinal`. A key containing a space must be quoted (the host surface's string
  quoting); a literal metacharacter is backslash-escaped
  ([escaping](::language:pointers:escaping)). The slash is an ordinary key character:
  `text/html` rides bare, and `*/pets` is a (dangling) reference to the literal key
  "/pets", never a rooted path.
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  deref    = "*" pointer          ; dereference — a graph edge (pull)
  define   = "&" pointer [ "[]" ] ; PATH anchor — also at that path
  pointer  = scope *( ":" portion )
  scope    = ":::" ws authority   ; the world
           / "::"                 ; current project root
           / ":"                  ; current document root
           / ".."                 ; parent node
           / portion              ; a key in the current mapping
  portion  = ( name / ".." ) *( index / relindex )
           / 1*( index / relindex )  ; a bare position: *[1], *[.-1]
  index    = "[" 1*DIGIT "]"      ; the integer key n (a position)
  relindex = "[" "." [("+"/"-") 1*DIGIT] "]"  ; host-relative
  name     = 1*( nchar / "\" CHAR ) / quoted
  nchar    = <any char except unescaped
              : [ ] * & # ~ ? ! ( ) < > = |  or whitespace>
- |
  The static **arity rule** classifies every expression by its form alone. If every
  portion guarantees at most one successor, the expression is a **link** — legal after
  `*` (an edge) and after `&` (an anchor). If any portion may fan out — a wildcard, a
  matcher, a descent, a fan-out uplink — it is a **query**: askable through the
  engine, never authorable as an edge; `something: *: team: ?` is a parse-time error.
  The classification is syntax-only today and designed to grow schema-aware — a schema
  proving "exactly one parent holds this relation" could admit an uplink into links —
  without moving the decision point.
